From cf0771595bd3d53c18dacdf63dd05d5435c57e8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aadi Takle <aadiatakle@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 10:57:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] feat(demos): retire the play gate on types with no
 transient stimulus
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The gate made the response clock start on a play press so M-RT measured
thinking rather than reading. That is worth a click only when the child
must be ready before something runs. For the other 43 types it is pure
friction between reading the instructions and answering.

The gate is still armed exactly as before and then released immediately
when the host sends `start`, rather than being removed. Two reasons:
several demos set state in openGate that their begin function requires
(VER-CLOZE-01's beginItem bails unless openGate has set gateOpen), and
the release has to happen on `start`, not `init` — beginning at init
fires the begin-time telemetry before anything is listening, which broke
four SPA types until the release was moved.

Release goes through #play because each demo names its begin function
differently (beginItem / startItem / begin) but all of them wire it to
that button.

Still gated, because the stimulus is transient and cannot be re-read:
QUANT-DOTS-01 (arrays flash for exposureMs then hide), WM-corsi-01,
WM-bind-01, WM-bubble-01, GB-TRACK-01.

Known gap, being addressed next: M-RTFIRST is declared by 37 types but
implemented in only 5 demos, so for now M-RT on the ungated types folds
reading into thinking. That separation returns when first-action latency
is instrumented.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
